<!-- ═══ COURSE STRUCTURE ═══ -->
<div class="section" id="course-structure">
  <div class="section-narrow">

    <h2 class="reveal">How the courses are structured</h2>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>How many sessions are there?</h3>
      <p>Eight live sessions, normally twice a week and completed within four weeks of your first session. The advanced <a href="/online-courses/eight-extraordinary-meridians/">Eight Extraordinary Meridians</a> course runs to ten sessions over five weeks.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>Why only twice a week? Couldn't I learn faster?</h3>
      <p>Deliberately, no. The space between sessions is where the practice actually takes root. Information can be delivered quickly; skill cannot. Two sessions a week gives you time to stand, to notice things, and to bring real questions to the next session.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>Which course should I begin with?</h3>
      <p>For most people, the ideal place to begin is one of the Five Element Qigong sets, such as <a href="/online-courses/xiu-jin/">Xiu Jin</a>, <a href="/online-courses/heaven-and-earth/">Heaven and Earth</a> or <a href="/online-courses/bagua-chun-gen/">Bagua Chun Gen</a>. <a href="/online-courses/zhan-zhuang/">Zhan Zhuang</a> standing practice is also open to complete beginners. Qigong that works with the <a href="/online-courses/eight-extraordinary-meridians/">Eight Extraordinary Meridians</a> or with Shen comes later, as it cannot be practised properly until you have developed reasonable skill in working with the Five Elements. Just tell me what you're after and I'll point you in the right direction.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER 2 - file: img/faq-online-2.jpg -->
    <figure class="faq-media reveal">
      <img src="/img/faq-online-2.jpg" alt="Zhan Zhuang standing practice in a live online session" loading="lazy" onerror="this.closest('figure').style.display='none'">
      <figcaption>Zhan Zhuang standing practice, taught live online</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>Can I pause partway through if life gets busy?</h3>
      <p>Yes. The second half of a course can be postponed for up to around three months, and support continues during the gap: calls to clarify anything arising in your practice, and further material sent your way.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>What happens once the course finishes?</h3>
      <p>I continue to support you for two months after your final session, answering questions and giving feedback on your practice, so you're not left to drift once the sessions end.</p>
    </div>

  </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══ SUITABILITY ═══ -->
<div class="section section--alt" id="suitability">
  <div class="section-narrow">

    <h2 class="reveal">Experience, background and suitability</h2>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>Do I need any previous experience?</h3>
      <p>None at all. Complete beginners are welcome. What matters far more than experience is patience, attention and a willingness to practise between sessions.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>I've already done some Qigong or Taiji. Will this repeat what I know?</h3>
      <p>Almost certainly not, and where it does overlap it will go considerably deeper. Much of the Qigong taught today is external shape with the internal content stripped out. The work here sits in a clear Traditional Chinese Medicine framework: meridians, acupoints, the Five Elements, the cultivation of Jing, Qi and Shen, and focuses on using the mind to guide the Qi rather than on choreography. Practitioners whose standing has never been properly checked often find the first sessions eye-opening.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>Am I too old, too stiff or too unfit for this?</h3>
      <p>Very unlikely. The movement involved is small, the demands are gradual, and everything is adjusted to what your body can do today rather than what it should be able to do. This is a practice aimed at longevity, not at looking impressive.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>Do I have to take it all very seriously?</h3>
      <p>Sincerity is needed; solemnity is not. I'll ask you to learn a few acupuncture points and meridian pathways and to refine your posture carefully, which is easier than it sounds. A light heart is genuinely part of the method. The Qi flows more freely when you aren't gritting your teeth.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>Can I carry on with my yoga, martial arts or other practice alongside this?</h3>
      <p>Yes, and most people find it improves them. Real Qigong tends to upgrade whatever else you do, because structure, root and relaxation carry across into everything.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>Is any of this religious?</h3>
            <p>No. The framework is Traditional Chinese Medicine and classical internal practice, and no belief is required. You also won't find recycled Daoist, Buddhist or self-help quotations here. They add nothing to your practice and only complicate something that is, at heart, very simple and accessible. All I ask of you is honest practice and an open mind.</p>
    </div>

  </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══ HEALTH AND SAFETY ═══ -->
<div class="section" id="health-safety">
  <div class="section-narrow">

    <h2 class="reveal">Your body, health and safety</h2>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>Taiji hurt my knees. Is standing practice going to make things worse?</h3>
      <p>It's a very common story, and the cause is usually the order in which the work is done. The knees need conditioning through specific preparatory practices before any deep or loaded work is attempted. In this Qigong the knees are unlocked rather than loaded, the movement involved is very small, and the deep bending and low stances seen all over the internet are simply not part of it. If you have a history of knee trouble, say so at the outset and I'll take that side of things carefully and at your pace.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>I have a health condition or an old injury. Should I mention it?</h3>
      <p>Yes, please do, before you begin. I work around contraindications and conditions needing adjustment as a matter of course, and it is far easier to shape the practice around something known from the start than to unpick a problem later.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>Is standing practice physically hard?</h3>
      <p>From the outside, nothing appears to happen. Inside, it is demanding in a very particular way. But duration is built up honestly, from a few genuine minutes upwards, and endurance for its own sake is not the aim. I will never ask you to hold a posture that puts you at risk, and I'll stop you if you try.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>What if I can't stand for long?</h3>
      <p>Then you stand for less, and build from there. Some postures can also be adapted. The structure of the course adjusts to you rather than the other way round.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>Is Qigong a substitute for medical treatment?</h3>
      <p>No. Qigong is recognised as one of the main branches of Traditional Chinese Medicine and the exercises I teach are medicinal in nature, working with the meridians to reduce excess, nourish deficiency and clear stagnation. That said, it sits alongside proper medical care rather than replacing it, and you should keep your doctor in the picture for anything serious.</p>
    </div>

  </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══ WHAT TO EXPECT ═══ -->
<div class="section section--alt" id="what-to-expect">
  <div class="section-narrow">

    <h2 class="reveal">What to expect from the practice</h2>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>Will I actually feel anything, and how soon?</h3>
      <p>Most students notice something fairly early: warmth, a sense of fullness in the hands, a settling of the breath, legs that are working harder than they look. Sensitivity of this kind is trained rather than imagined, and it grows with consistency. How quickly it develops varies from person to person, and honest practice between sessions is the single biggest factor.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- VIDEO PLACEHOLDER 2 - file: img/faq-online-2.mp4 -->
    <figure class="faq-media reveal">
      <video src="/img/faq-online-2.mp4#t=0.1" controls playsinline preload="metadata" onerror="this.closest('figure').style.display='none'"></video>
      <figcaption>Guidance and correction during a live online session</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>Isn't standing still rather dull?</h3>
      <p>It sounds that way until you try it properly. Standing is one of the most demanding and rewarding practices in the whole of Qigong, and there is a great deal going on once you know what to pay attention to. Boredom usually means nobody has shown you where to look.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>How is this different from the Qigong I see online?</h3>
      <p>Most of what circulates is gentle, flowing movement with the internal content removed, wrapped in recycled philosophical quotations. The work here is the opposite: specific, precise and internal, with the mind actively guiding the energy. It gives the body something concrete to do and the mind something it can verify.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item reveal">
      <h3>How do I get started?</h3>
      <p>Register your interest through the <a href="/online-courses/#register">online courses page</a>, or get in touch directly by <a href="mailto:info@innerflowing.org">email</a> or through the <a href="/contact/">contact form</a>. Mention your experience so far, anything physical that needs taking into account, and what you're hoping to get from the practice, and I'll advise you on the most sensible place to begin.</p>
    </div>

